{
  "id": "negation",
  "name": "Negatives",
  "level": "A2",
  "subtitle": "Make a sentence negative with not. With be and can, say isn't, aren't, can't. With other verbs, use don't / doesn't / didn't + the base verb: I don't like it. She didn't go.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "isn't",
    "can't",
    "don't / doesn't / didn't",
    "base verb"
  ],
  "source": "icanspeak Spanish original English text (grammar-a2 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "I / she / they …"
      },
      {
        "text": "don't / doesn't / didn't",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "base verb",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "rest"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "I <span class=\"vp-verb\">don't</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">like</span> coffee. · She <span class=\"vp-verb\">doesn't</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">work</span> here. · We <span class=\"vp-verb\">didn't</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">go</span>. · He <span class=\"vp-verb\">isn't</span> at home.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "With <strong>be</strong>, add <em>not</em> after the verb: <em>I'm not tired. She isn't here. They aren't ready. It wasn't cold. We weren't late.</em> Do not use <em>do</em> with be.",
        "tag": "be"
      },
      {
        "html": "With <strong>can, will, should, must</strong> (modal verbs), add <em>not</em>: <em>can't, won't, shouldn't, mustn't</em>. Then use the base verb: <em>I can't swim. He won't come.</em>",
        "tag": "can't / won't"
      },
      {
        "html": "With <strong>all other verbs</strong> in the present, use <em>don't</em> (I, you, we, they) or <em>doesn't</em> (he, she, it) + base verb: <em>I don't like fish. She doesn't work here.</em> The base verb has no <em>-s</em>.",
        "tag": "don't / doesn't"
      },
      {
        "html": "In the past, use <em>didn't</em> + base verb for every person: <em>I didn't see him. They didn't go.</em> The verb does <em>not</em> change: not <em>didn't went</em>.",
        "tag": "didn't"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>no</strong> or <strong>not</strong>? Use <em>no</em> before a noun, with the meaning \"not any\": <em>I have no time. There's no milk.</em> Use <em>not</em> to make a verb negative: <em>I don't have any time.</em> Both can mean the same: <em>There's no milk. = There isn't any milk.</em>",
        "tag": "no or not?"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>I no like coffee.</em> → ✓ <em>I don't like coffee.</em> · ✗ <em>He don't work here.</em> → ✓ <em>He doesn't work here.</em> · ✗ <em>She didn't went home.</em> → ✓ <em>She didn't go home.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "In speaking and in emails, use the short forms: <em>don't, isn't, can't</em>. The full forms (<em>do not, is not</em>) are for formal writing or for emphasis. Short answers use short forms too: <em>No, I don't. No, she isn't.</em> For <em>some / any / no</em> and words like <em>nobody</em> and <em>nothing</em>, see <a href=\"/some-any\">some / any / no</a>. For <em>can, must, should</em>, see <a href=\"/modal-verbs\">Modal verbs</a>. For the past forms, see <a href=\"/past-simple\">Past simple</a>.",
